course: books
lesson: 61
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter X, part 1: The Further Adventures of Toad (free, the canal and the barge-woman's washing)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Toad wakes in the hollow tree where he hid after jumping off the train. His toes are freezing, and he dreams that his bedclothes have run downstairs to the kitchen fire to get warm. Then he remembers the best thing of all: he is free! «The word and the thought alone were worth fifty blankets.»
explain: It is an early summer morning and he has the whole world to himself, but nothing can tell him the way home. He finds a road, then a canal running beside it, and he thinks: they must both be coming from somewhere and going to somewhere. So he follows the water.
explain: A horse comes along the tow-path pulling a barge, steered by a big stout woman in a sun-bonnet. Toad, still dressed as a washerwoman, tells her a sad made-up story: a married daughter in trouble near Toad Hall, his washing business left behind, all his money lost. She kindly offers him a lift, and Toad thinks: «Toad's luck again! I always come out on top!»
explain: But Toad boasts too much. He says he loves washing and is never so happy as with both arms in the wash-tub — so the barge-woman gives him a heap of her washing to do. He tries coaxing it, slapping it, punching it; his back aches, his proud paws go crinkly, he loses the soap for the fiftieth time. Then he hears loud laughter: she has been watching him all along.
fact: Toad slept in a hollow tree whose «front door» faced east, so the early sun woke him, along with his very cold toes.
fact: He had slept for weeks «on straw over stone flags» in prison, so he had almost forgotten how nice thick blankets are.
fact: «Free! The word and the thought alone were worth fifty blankets.» (worth fifty blankets = it warmed him more than fifty blankets could)
fact: The road seemed «like a stray dog, to be looking anxiously for company»; then a canal joined it «like a shy little brother».
fact: Toad's reasoning: «They must both be coming from somewhere, and going to somewhere. You can't get over that.» (you can't get over that = nobody can argue with that)
fact: A barge is a long flat boat on a canal; here a horse walking on the tow-path (the path beside the canal) pulls it with a long rope, and the barge-woman steers with the tiller (the handle of the rudder).
fact: Toad, in the washerwoman's dress, lies to her: his married daughter has sent for him, he is «in the washing and laundering line», he has left his children and lost all his money.
fact: The barge-woman is going past Toad Hall herself: the canal joins the river «a little above Toad Hall», so she gives him a lift.
fact: Toad boasts: «Finest business in the whole country», twenty girls working for him, and «Never so happy as when I've got both arms in the wash-tub.» He also calls his workers rude names — a nasty, snobbish way to talk.
fact: Her husband has gone off with the dog to catch a rabbit for dinner and is supposed to meet her at the next lock (a lock is a section of canal with gates that lifts or lowers boats).
fact: She asks Toad to wash some of her things; Toad, frightened, offers to steer instead, but she says «It takes some practice to steer a barge properly.»
fact: «If it comes to that,» he thought in desperation, «I suppose any fool can wash!» He tries coaxing, slapping and punching the clothes; nothing works.
fact: Old words: «I dare say» = I suppose; «post-haste» = in a great hurry; «humbug» = a fake, a pretender; «a dish-clout» = a dishcloth; «I'll lay» = I bet.
fact: The barge-woman laughs till she cries: «I thought you must be a humbug all along, from the conceited way you talked.»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bare» (London) = lots of; «canny» (North East England) = quite, or clever; «sus» = suspicious; «lowkey» = a bit, secretly; «yeah nah» (Australia) = no.
exercise: Where did Toad sleep the night after he jumped from the train? | In a hollow tree in the wood | Under a hedge by the railway | In a barn full of hay | In the engine-driver's cab
exercise: Which way did the «front door» of the hollow tree face? | East, so the sun woke him early | North, towards the cold castle and its dungeon | West | Towards the railway tunnel
exercise: What did Toad dream about his bedclothes? | They had run downstairs to the kitchen fire to warm themselves | They had turned into a washerwoman's gown | They had flown out of the window | They had been carried off by the warders and the policemen while he slept
exercise: «Worth fifty blankets.» What was worth fifty blankets to Toad? | The word and the thought «Free!» | A hot breakfast | His own warm bed at Toad Hall, with the Tudor window | Money in his pocket
exercise: Why had Toad almost forgotten how nice thick blankets are? | He had slept for weeks on straw over stone | He had never once in his life slept under a blanket | The tree was very dark inside | An owl kept watch over him
exercise: What season is it now? | Early summer | Deep winter, with snow in the Wild Wood | Autumn, with falling leaves | Early spring, at spring-cleaning time
exercise: What did Toad want to find first that morning? | Something that could talk and tell him the way | Something to eat, as hot as possible | A motor-car | A deep, dark place where he could hide all day until night came
exercise: What joined the road «like a shy little brother»? | A canal | A railway line | A river with willows on its banks | A narrow country lane
exercise: «They must both be coming from somewhere, and going to somewhere.» So what did Toad decide to do? | Follow the water along the canal | Sit and wait for a cart | Go back to the wood | Walk the opposite way, away from towns and policemen
exercise: What came round the bend in the canal first? | A horse, pulling a long rope | A boat full of policemen in blue | The barge-woman's husband and his dog | A fox trotting along the tow-path
exercise: What was the barge-woman wearing on her head? | A linen sun-bonnet | A rusty black bonnet | A straw hat with flowers | A woollen shawl and cap
exercise: Which family story did Toad invent for her? | His married daughter had sent for him to come at once | His husband was ill in hospital | His little son was lost on the river somewhere near the big weir | His house had burnt down
exercise: Where did Toad say his married daughter lived? | Near the river, close to Toad Hall | In the old town with the grim castle and the prison | In London | By the railway station
exercise: Why could the barge-woman give him a lift? | Her canal joins the river a little above Toad Hall | She was taking a load of coal to the gaol in the castle town | She knew he was Toad | Her husband told her to
exercise: «Toad's luck again! I always come out on top!» What does «come out on top» mean? | Win in the end | Climb to the top of the boat | Float on water | Wake up early in the morning
exercise: How many girls did Toad say worked for him? | Twenty or thereabouts | Two | A hundred | None, he said he did all the work alone
exercise: Where had the barge-woman's husband gone? | Off with the dog to catch a rabbit for dinner | To the pub, with all their money | To Toad Hall to sell vegetables and eggs | Fishing
exercise: What did Toad offer to do instead of the washing? | Steer the barge | Cook the rabbit for dinner | Lead the horse along the tow-path | Sing to her while she works
exercise: Why wouldn't she let him steer? | It takes practice to steer a barge properly | He was too small to reach the tiller | The horse would not obey a stranger | She did not trust him
exercise: «If it comes to that, I suppose any fool can wash!» What did Toad find out? | Washing is not as easy as it looks | Washing is the easiest job in the world, and a real pleasure | Any fool can steer | Soap floats
exercise: What three ways did Toad try on the clothes? | Coaxing, slapping and punching | Boiling, ironing and folding them neatly | Singing, dancing and shouting | Rubbing, rinsing and wringing, like a real washerwoman
exercise: What worried Toad about his paws? | They were getting all crinkly | They were turning blue | He had lost his gold rings in the soapy water | They were bleeding
exercise: How many times had he lost the soap by the end? | The fiftieth time | Only once, at the very start | The second time he tried | Never once, he held it tight
exercise: «I thought you must be a humbug all along.» What does «humbug» mean? | A fake, a pretender | A grumpy, cross person | A kind of black beetle | A kind of flat barge
test: Jade, lowkey (a bit) confused: «so Toad slept in a nice inn that night, yeah?» Put it right. | No, he slept in a hollow tree | Yes, the kind engine-driver paid for a room for him at the inn | No, he slept on the train all night | Yes, at the Red Lion
test: «I dare say it is, ma'am!» What does «I dare say» mean here? | I suppose | I am brave enough to say | I don't believe it | I dare you
test: Why does Toad talk about «my married daughter» and «my young children»? | He is pretending to be a washerwoman, so he invents a family | He really has a wife, a daughter and children that nobody in the book knew about | He is talking about the field-mice | He is joking with Rat
test: Kai: «Toad's lie about loving washing was a canny (clever) move, it got him a lift, end of.» What really happened? | The lift was given before; the boasting about washing got him a heap of washing to do | It worked perfectly and she let him steer | It got him a free dinner | She paid him for his washing
test: What would have happened if Toad had said he hated washing? | He would probably have sat quietly and enjoyed the lift | He would have been thrown straight into the canal by the barge-woman at once | He would have been arrested | He would have had to steer
test: Why does Toad say «I'm more used to gentlemen's things myself»? | He is trying to get out of washing her clothes | He really washes gentlemen's shirts at Toad Hall | He likes ironing | He wants a job
test: True or false? «The barge-woman believed Toad all the way through.» | False: she says she thought he was a humbug all along | True: she was completely fooled until the very end | True: she never laughed | False: she knew at once he was Toad
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, who helped Toad get the washerwoman's dress? | The gaoler's daughter, whose aunt was a washerwoman | The engine-driver | Badger, who sent it in secretly with a parcel of food | The Chief Weasel
test: Looking back: why did Toad have no money on the train platform in chapter VIII? | He left his coat and waistcoat, with his money, in his cell | The aunt took it all | A fox stole it | He spent it on toast
test: Looking back: what had Toad promised the engine-driver, though he had «never washed a shirt in his life»? | To wash some of his shirts | To pay for a ticket later | To drive the engine | To find his children
test: Looking back: what did the fox say to Toad in the wood at the end of chapter VIII? | «Hullo, washerwoman! Half a pair of socks and a pillow-case short this week!» | «Hullo, Toad! Off to the Wild Wood?» | «Mind the owl!» | «Want to sell that horse?»
test: Looking back: in chapter I, the Rat said there is nothing «half so much worth doing as» what? | «Simply messing about in boats» | Washing clothes on a barge | Driving a motor-car very fast | Writing poems about ducks
test: Sam, yeah nah (no) not buying it: «Toad's well humble now after prison.» Which line from this part proves him wrong? | «Toad's luck again! I always come out on top!» | «I am a poor unhappy washerwoman» | «Bother them!» | «A nice morning, ma'am!»
test: Why might Toad be so desperate to reach Toad Hall quickly? | He has no money, he is hungry, and the police may be hunting him | He wants to wash clothes there | He has a great party at Toad Hall that very night, with music and speeches and songs | He wants to see the barge-woman's daughter
test: ★★★ «A long half-hour passed.» If Toad lost the soap 50 times in 30 minutes, about how often did he lose it? | More than once a minute | Once every ten minutes | Once every half-hour | Once every two minutes
test: ★★★ The road is «like a stray dog» and the canal «a shy little brother». Which word describes how the author treats the road and canal here? | As if they were people or animals with feelings | As if they were a map | As if they were dangerous enemies | As if they were only lifeless water and stones, with no feelings of any kind
test: ★★★ Which of these did Toad NOT tell the barge-woman? | That he had escaped from prison | That he was in the washing line | That his married daughter had sent for him | That he had lost all his money
exam: Identify the circumstance which, by Toad's own reckoning, was «worth fifty blankets» upon his awakening. | The realisation that he was free | The warmth of the morning sun upon his toes | The proximity of the canal | The possession of a hot breakfast
exam: It is given that the barge-woman declined Toad's offer to steer. Ascertain the reason she advanced. | That steering a barge requires practice | That the horse would not tolerate him | That steering was prohibited to passengers by law | That her husband would shortly return
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the consequence of Toad's assertion that he delighted in washing. | He was set to wash the barge-woman's garments | He was remunerated handsomely for his services as a laundress | He was permitted to steer the vessel | He was set down at Toad Hall forthwith
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the barge-woman's opinion of Toad? | She had suspected him of being a humbug from the outset, owing to his conceited manner of speech | She believed him a genuine laundress until he spoke | She mistook him for the gaoler's daughter | She regarded him as a man of rank
